Q: If ... continue VS If ... elseif A: <p>The two codes in their current version are functionally equivalent. However, when you write code, you ought to think about future versions too. And from this perspective we see that the second code chunk is logically flawed, because it is possible that <code>!is_valid</code> will be true, <code>cnt</code> will be 0 and <code>item.freq &lt; min</code> would be true at the same time. It is true that the <code>continue;</code> happens to prevent latter cases from being executed, it makes the code slightly more difficult to read due to the jumping and whenever you touch this code you will need to worry about what logic you add and whether it still works, also, you duplicate the <code>continue;</code> part too.</p>
<p>The if-else is logically solid, as it clarifies these are different cases which may need different handling too. However, sometimes you may be able to handle cases together. In your initial version you can do:</p>
<pre><code>for (auto&amp; item : items)
{
    if (!((!item.is_valid) || (item.cnt == 0) || (item.freq &lt; min))) {
        Process(item);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>making the condition a one-liner, but this assumes you need nothing to do if the item is not to be processed. Of course, you can create an else branch, like</p>
<pre><code>for (auto&amp; item : items)
{
    if (!((!item.is_valid) || (item.cnt == 0) || (item.freq &lt; min))) {
        Process(item);
    } else {
        //your handler here
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>which allows you to handle them together. If you can handle them together, then all good. If not, then you could create a <code>validate</code> function that wour receive the <code>item</code> and <code>min</code> and handle your custom validation and errors, returning whether <code>item</code> is valid and then you can do:</p>
<pre><code>for (auto&amp; item : items)
{
    if (validate(item)) {
        Process(item);
    } else {
        //your handler here
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to correctly set up Next-Auth with Keycloak A: <p>You’re not overthinking. You’re seeing the normal behavior of two separate session systems:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>NextAuth session (cookie/JWT in your app)</p>
</li>
<li><p>Keycloak SSO session (at the IdP)</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>They are related, but not automatically “always in sync”.</p>
<h3>What’s happening</h3>
<ul>
<li><p><code>events.signOut</code> runs on explicit sign-out actions, not on passive expiry.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If NextAuth cookie expires naturally, there is no browser redirect to Keycloak logout.</p>
</li>
<li><p>With JWT sessions, NextAuth only talks to Keycloak again if you implement refresh logic in <code>jwt</code> callback (or user re-authenticates).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>NextAuth docs confirm:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>JWT callback is called on sign-in and when session is accessed.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Token expiry is extended while session is active.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Events are side-effects, not a session-sync engine.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Can you enforce “3h idle + 1 day absolute”?</h3>
<p>Yes, but configure both sides intentionally.</p>
<h2>Recommended model</h2>
<h3>1) Enforce hard rules in Keycloak (source of truth)</h3>
<p>Set in Keycloak (realm or client-level overrides):</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>Client Session Idle</code> = 3 hours</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>Client Session Max</code> = 24 hours<br />
(or SSO-level if you want it global)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Keycloak docs: session idle/max and client overrides are exactly for this.</p>
<h3>2) Match app session window in NextAuth</h3>
<p>Set NextAuth max to 24h, and use refresh-token flow so app notices Keycloak expiry quickly.</p>
<h3>3) Implement refresh in jwt callback</h3>
<p>Store <code>access_token</code>, <code>refresh_token</code>, <code>expires_at</code>, and refresh when needed.</p>
<p><code>[...nextauth].ts</code> (v4 style)</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import NextAuth from &quot;next-auth&quot;;
import KeycloakProvider from &quot;next-auth/providers/keycloak&quot;;

async function refreshAccessToken(token: any) {
  try {
    const url = `${process.env.KEYCLOAK_ISSUER}/protocol/openid-connect/token`;

    const res = await fetch(url, {
      method: &quot;POST&quot;,
      headers: { &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/x-www-form-urlencoded&quot; },
      body: new URLSearchParams({
        client_id: process.env.KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID!,
        client_secret: process.env.KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET!,
        grant_type: &quot;refresh_token&quot;,
        refresh_token: token.refreshToken,
      }),
    });

    const refreshed = await res.json();
    if (!res.ok) throw refreshed;

    return {
      ...token,
      accessToken: refreshed.access_token,
      accessTokenExpires: Date.now() + refreshed.expires_in * 1000,
      refreshToken: refreshed.refresh_token ?? token.refreshToken,
      idToken: refreshed.id_token ?? token.idToken,
      error: undefined,
    };
  } catch {
    return { ...token, error: &quot;RefreshAccessTokenError&quot; };
  }
}

export default NextAuth({
  providers: [
    KeycloakProvider({
      clientId: process.env.KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID!,
      clientSecret: process.env.KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET!,
      issuer: process.env.KEYCLOAK_ISSUER!, // &lt;--- INCLUDE /realms/&lt;realm&gt;
    }),
  ],
  session: {
    strategy: &quot;jwt&quot;,
    maxAge: 24 * 60 * 60, // hard app-session cap
  },
  callbacks: {
    async jwt({ token, account }) {
      // HERE GOES THE INITIAL SIGN_IN
      if (account) {
        return {
          ...token,
          accessToken: account.access_token,
          accessTokenExpires: (account.expires_at ?? 0) * 1000,
          refreshToken: account.refresh_token,
          idToken: account.id_token,
          authTime: Date.now(),
        };
      }

      // THIS IS AN OPTIONAL HARD LOCK
      if (token.authTime &amp;&amp; Date.now() - (token.authTime as number) &gt; 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000) {
        return { ...token, error: &quot;HardSessionExpired&quot; };
      }

      // ACCESS TOKEN VALID
      if (Date.now() &lt; (token.accessTokenExpires as number) - 30_000) return token;

      // THE REFRESH
      return refreshAccessToken(token);
    },
    async session({ session, token }) {
      (session as any).accessToken = token.accessToken;
      (session as any).error = token.error;
      return session;
    },
  },
});
</code></pre>
<ol start="4">
<li>Federated logout when user clicks sign out</li>
</ol>
<p>Use OIDC RP-initiated logout endpoint with id_token_hint and post_logout_redirect_uri.</p>
<p>Keycloak supports this endpoint and params.</p>
<h2>Important expectation</h2>
<p>Perfect real-time sync is not possible in all cases (e.g., cookie expires while tab is closed).<br />
What you can get is practical consistency:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Keycloak policy enforces true idle/max limits.</p>
</li>
<li><p>NextAuth refresh detects invalid Keycloak session and terminates app session.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Explicit sign-out logs out both app and Keycloak.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want, I can give you a full working <code>federated-logout</code> route + client <code>signOut()</code> flow for NextAuth v4 or Auth.js v5 (whichever you use).</p>

Q: Make a 3d image preview on my web site made of some images of the 3d object from different angles A: <p>The answer i found for who'd like is the following</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const threeDObj = document.querySelector(".threeDObj");
let mouseMoveListenerActive = false;

        function mouseDownListener() {
            if (!mouseMoveListenerActive) {
                threeDObj.addEventListener("mousemove", mouseMoveListener);
                mouseMoveListenerActive = true;
            }
            console.log("Mouse down");
            threeDObj.innerHTML = "Mouse down";
        }

        function mouseMoveListener(event) {
            console.log("Mouse position: " + event.clientX + ", " + event.clientY);
            threeDObj.innerHTML = "Mouse position: " + event.clientX + ", " + event.clientY;
        }

        function mouseUpListener() {
            if (mouseMoveListenerActive) {
                threeDObj.removeEventListener("mousemove", mouseMoveListener);
                mouseMoveListenerActive = false;
            }
            console.log("Mouse up");
            threeDObj.innerHTML = "Mouse up";
        }

        threeDObj.addEventListener("mousedown", mouseDownListener);
        threeDObj.addEventListener("mouseup", mouseUpListener);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.threeDObj {
            user-select: none;
            -webkit-user-drag: none;
            -moz-user-select: none;
            -webkit-user-select: none;
            width: 1000px;
            height: 1000px;
        }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>    &lt;p class="threeDObj"&gt;previous&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>now instead of changing the text i can change the image that i want it to appear based on 3x16 array of img src's</p>
